The V12 checkout model is a single-class, FIFO discrete-event model. It models one request queue with finite capacity: requests wait in arrival order, a free server dispatches the head of the queue, and work that cannot be admitted is rejected and counted. It does not model priority queue classes or preemption; the verification page explicitly records "the queue discipline is FIFO only; priority and preemption are not implemented," and a model needing them requires a new contract and tests. Separately, the event calendar orders simultaneous events by time, then priority (0 = service completion/timeout, 1 = arrival, 2 = retry), then insertion sequence; that is deterministic event ordering, not request prioritization.

The resource controls are:
- a fixed server pool (the baseline has 12 servers, 0.5 s mean service time, giving 24 requests/s capacity and 0.833 utilization at 20 requests/s);
- bounded queue capacity (maximum waiting requests);
- a bulkhead cap on waiting plus in-service requests;
- a per-attempt timeout (a one-second deadline in the checkout scenarios; the deadline is reset per attempt, not per request lifetime);
- a bounded retry budget with a maximum attempt count, backoff (none, exponential or linear) and jitter (none, full or equal);
- admission control: when the queue or bulkhead limit is reached the request is rejected and the rejection is recorded rather than silently blocked. In the A/B/C comparison, A has no retry budget, B has three attempts with exponential backoff and full jitter, and C has two attempts with linear backoff. The overload case (40-request queue, one-second deadline) shows timeout as the dominant loss (0.3495) rather than rejection (0.0005).
